 the junction of project leadership and innovation","type":"Book","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-565659","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"When organizations realize that they need to innovate, they often have their knowledge workers participate in inter-organizational “knowledge networks” that have the purpose of developing their participants’ skills and competencies through facilitated meetings. The main problem of knowledge networks is that it can be difficult to evaluate whether the network group is “healthy” and follows its purpose and whether its participants gain any value as a result, so the design problem faced in this study was “How to design a tool to assist network coordinators with the continuous development of network groups.” The problem was broken into three sub-problems: identifying the types of knowledge networks, identifying a tool for gauging a knowledge network’s “health,” and identifying a process through which knowledge networks can be effectively established, maintained, and ended. The problem was complicated by the need to identify common interests among the knowledge networks’ main stakeholders, for whom the solution had to provide value. The stakeholder groups were identified as network sponsors, network facilitators, and network participants. Three artifacts were designed to solve the problems identified. Artifact 1 was a visualization of the process of how to establish, maintain, operate, and evaluate and/or end a knowledge network. To support this process, two additional interactive artifacts were designed. The second artifact was a document called a “network charter” to be used by the facilitator and network participants at the beginning of and during the knowledge network process. The third artifact was an assessment tool for assessing seven key parameters of the selected knowledge network using a radar chart. Three main lessons were learned in the DSR project. First, we found that the DSR approach can be beneficial in creating new kernel theories, not just design theory. The concept of knowledge network archetypes was extracted through a combination of a literature review on knowledge networks and through the empirical activities involved in uncovering participant value and network facilitators’ evaluation of the artifacts. Second, we learned that designing artifacts that provide value to various stakeholders with asymmetric power relationship on multiple levels should be pursued by DSR researchers. Third, DSR can be used to provide situational solutions, not just normative ones.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Hansen, Magnus Rotvit Perlt","Pries-Heje, Jan"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-569860"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Cases","Design science research"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-RUC"]},"level3all":["DI-U-RUC"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a situational knowledge network nexus building approach kernel theory extensions using design science research","src":["ruc"],"src-code":["DI-U-RUC"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-569860","ids":["ruc-ceafc56e-5d39-47e8-96b0-fa679e0214d8"],"keyword":["Cases","Design Science Research"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-609F81F28B7FC7EDCB74B40D08BD56CE"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ruc roskilde university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-RUC","l3nl":"Roskilde University","l3nn":"RUC Roskilde University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"RUC Roskilde University","namevar":["Roskilde University, Department of People and Technology","Roskilde University, Department of People and Technology, Sustainable Digitalization"],"nora_code":"DI-U-RUC","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","first":"Magnus Rotvit Perlt","key":"hansenpau","last":"Hansen","locid":"d9c2134a-0799-45eb-8a15-e0523fc96da2","name":"Hansen, Magnus Rotvit Perlt","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","country":"dk","first":"Jan","key":"prieshejepau","last":"Pries-Heje","locid":"6dbca1cc-ed5b-479e-be59-0cf0ae86a2a8","name":"Pries-Heje, Jan","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Jan","key":"vombrockepedh","last":"Vom Brocke","name":"Vom Brocke, Jan","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Alan","key":"hevnerpedh","last":"Hevner","name":"Hevner, Alan","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Alexander","key":"maedchepedh","last":"Maedche","name":"Maedche, Alexander","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-030-46781-4_11","eisbn":["9783030467814"],"isbn":["9783030467807"],"issn":["21968705"],"pages":"261-288","publisher":"Springer","series":"Progress in IS","status":"p","subtitle":"Cases","title":"Design Science Research"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Roskilde University","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ruc-ceafc56e-5d39-47e8-96b0-fa679e0214d8"],"src":["RUC Roskilde University"],"title":"A Situational Knowledge Network Nexus Building Approach : Kernel Theory Extensions using Design Science Research","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-569860","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"author_count":3,"contributors":["Tchatchoua, Nadine Sandjo","Pries-Heje, Jan","Hansen, Magnus Rotvit Perlt"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-557499"],"event":{"end":"2020-08-12","place":"Sundsvall, se","start":"2020-08-09","title":"43rd Information Systems Research in Scandinavia Conference (Online)","year":"2020"},"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-RUC"]},"level3all":["DI-U-RUC"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"changing household behaviour to use electricity from sustainable sources","src":["ruc"],"src-code":["DI-U-RUC"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-557499","ids":["ruc-168899d0-7919-46a5-a706-76245c4e2a79"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-E0460B3DD8532FF36FC2DA674D18E553"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ruc roskilde university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-RUC","l3nl":"Roskilde University","l3nn":"RUC Roskilde University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"RUC Roskilde University","namevar":["Roskilde University, Department of People and Technology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-RUC","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","first":"Nadine Sandjo","key":"tchatchouapau","last":"Tchatchoua","locid":"00d16de9-68e1-4fbb-95c4-4198b1dde7f0","name":"Tchatchoua, Nadine Sandjo","orcid":"0000-0001-5638-147X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","country":"dk","first":"Jan","key":"prieshejepau","last":"Pries-Heje","locid":"6dbca1cc-ed5b-479e-be59-0cf0ae86a2a8","name":"Pries-Heje, Jan","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","country":"dk","first":"Magnus Rotvit Perlt","key":"hansenpau","last":"Hansen","locid":"d9c2134a-0799-45eb-8a15-e0523fc96da2","name":"Hansen, Magnus Rotvit Perlt","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Roskilde University","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ruc-168899d0-7919-46a5-a706-76245c4e2a79"],"src":["RUC Roskilde University"],"title":"Changing household behaviour to use electricity from sustainable sources","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-557499","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Project Portfolio Management (PPM) focuses on the integration and alignment of projects with the business operation in order to achieve most value and cost-efficiency for the investment in projects. PPM is often a challenge and especially so for improvement projects where PPM is considerably underdeveloped. In this paper, we present a model for Project Portfolio Improvement combining predicted success and expected value. The core of the model is about measuring expected value up against a measure of the predicted project success rate. Value can for example be measured as earnings/profit, maturity, and/or efficiency. Success can be measured using the ImprovAbility model that is part of ISO 33014. We then present a case from Vestas, one of the leading windmill producers in the world. Vestas have used the model for project portfolio management in three rounds in 2016-19 to improve their abilities and increase the rate of success dramatically. Finally, we conclude that our generic model may be very useful for process improvement and innovation PPM in other companies.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Pries-Heje, Jan","Johansen, Jørn","Jakobsen, Peter Møller"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-559965"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-PR","DI-U"],"level3":{"PR":["DI-PR-ALL","DI-PR-OTH","DI-PR-VES"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-RUC"]},"level3all":["DI-PR-OTH","DI-PR-VES","DI-U-RUC"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"project portfolio improvability measuring expected value against predicted success in order to decide and improve the project portfolio","src":["ruc"],"src-code":["DI-U-RUC"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-559965","ids":["ruc-3b223269-92e5-49d4-9d44-38ee1e289163"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-0175F