ervillepau","last":"Baskerville","name":"Baskerville, Richard","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"isbn":["9783319095462"],"issn":["18651348"],"publisher":"Springer","series":"Lecture Notes in Business Information Processing","status":"p","title":"Lecture Notes in Business Information Processing: 5th Scandinavian Conference on Information Systems, Scis 2014"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84927590655"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Roskilde University","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-d1460d41-220d-4d9d-beee-fe6c69e37fac"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Designing Scientific Creativity","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-828178","year":"2014","yearsub":"2014"}
{"abstract":"A better understanding of how interorganizational information systems (IOIS) are adopted is important as a successful implementation of information systems (IS) can be expected to raise long-term organizational efficiency. However, organizations often find IOIS adoption to be a bumpy ride, and despite the apparent reason to come to terms with IOIS, the utilization rate is still low. Adoption of IOIS is an interesting process to study, because of the high complexity in successful adoption of IOIS created by the increased number of organizations involved in the adoption process, and because of the many different explanations for adopting or rejecting IOIS offered in the wide body of literature. This literature review found four different streams of research; 1) studies explaining why organizations adopt or reject IOIS by investigating factors determining adoption of or barriers to IOIS adoption; 2) studies explaining how to create the desired organizational effects with IOIS; 3) studies explaining how IOIS are affecting the buyer-supplier relationship they are introduced into; and 4) studies categorizing the structure of IOIS adopters in different configurations. Seemingly only limited effort has been made to study the actual adoption process. This apparent lack of studies of the adoption process calls for research opening the black box of IOIS adoption. Key words: Interorganizational information systems, adoption, literature review","author_count":4,"contributors":["Brandt, Charlotte J."],"cuid":["S02-083.38-021595"],"event":{"acronym":"IRIS","end":"2014-08-13","place":"Ringsted, dk","start":"2014-08-10","title":"Scandinavian Conference in Information Systems (SCIS 5 / IRIS 37)","year":"2014"},"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"toward a process view in adoption of interorganizational information systems a literature review","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"http://iris2014.aau.dk/","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S02-083.38-021595","ids":["au-7a57fa07-94ab-40a4-889f-aa509caf7b10","au-aafbd1fd-6d75-4d4c-98ae-ed4898be83b4"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Local-1-2-32B4A4FC8877CB00D6BD877FD3BA2E72"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"http://iris2014.aau.dk/","version":"Other Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Blocked","mra":["Social Science (SOC)"],"yearPub":"2014","yearSub":"2014"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Aarhus BSS, Department of Management"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Charlotte J.","key":"brandtpau","last":"Brandt","locid":"6f2e7dc0-4428-41d1-8a70-de85f63c0f06","name":"Brandt, Charlotte J.","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Ahmad","key":"ghazawnehpedh","last":"Ghazawneh","name":"Ghazawneh, Ahmad","rank":2,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Jacob","key":"nørbjergpedh","last":"Nørbjerg","name":"Nørbjerg, Jacob","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Jan","key":"prieshejepedh","last":"Pries-Heje","name":"Pries-Heje, Jan","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"eisbn":["9788773498767 (USB)"],"isbn":["9788773498767"],"issn":["18919863"],"series":"IRIS: Selected Papers of the Information Systems Research Seminar in Scandinavia","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 37th Information Systems Research Seminar in Scandinavia (IRIS 37)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-7a57fa07-94ab-40a4-889f-aa509caf7b10","au-aafbd1fd-6d75-4d4c-98ae-ed4898be83b4"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Toward a Process View in Adoption of Interorganizational Information Systems : A Literature Review","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-083.38-021595","year":"2014","yearsub":"2014"}
{"abstract":"Both the practice and the research literature on information systems attach great value to the identification and dissemination of information on “best practices”. In the philosophy of science, this type of knowledge is regarded as technological knowledge because it becomes manifest in the successful techniques in one context. While the value for other contexts is unproven, knowledge of best practices circulates under an assumption that the practices will usefully self-diffuse through innovation and adoption in other contexts. We study diffusion of best practices using a design science approach. The study context is a design case in which an organization desires to diffuse its best practices across different groups. The design goal is embodied in organizational mechanisms to achieve this diffusion. The study used Theory of Planned Behavior (TPB) as a kernel theory. The artifacts resulting from the design were two-day training workshops conceptually anchored to TBP. The design theory was evaluated through execution of eight diffusion workshops involving three different groups in the same company. The findings indicate that the match between the practice and the context materialized in the presence of two concordant factors. On the context side, the qualities of the selected opinion leader were necessary to provide the subjective norm described in TPB. On the best practice side, the technological qualities of the best practice itself were necessary to instill the ideal attitude (belief that the behavior will be effective). These two factors were especially critical if the source context of the best practice is qualitatively different from the target context into which the organization is seeking to diffuse the best practice.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Pries-Heje, Jan","Baskerville, Richard"],"cuid":["S02-091.30-038350"],"event":{"acronym":"IFIP WG86","end":"2014-06-04","place":"Aalborg, dk","start":"2014-06-02","title":"IFIP WG 8.6 International Conference on Transfer and Diffusion of IT","year":"2014"},"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Georgia State University"],"keyword":["Best practices","Diffusion of Innovation","Organizational Mechanisms","Technological Knowledge","Theory of Planned Behavior TPB"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum","sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-RUC"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-RUC"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"diffusing best practices a design science study using the theory of planed behavior","src":["itu","ruc"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-RUC"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/84927670859","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S02-091.30-038350","identifier":[{"id":"84927670859","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-961f1027-c258-4baa-b827-e889ac0ca00e","ruc-d9e7a194-08fa-4bb4-ae7f-e0e127d24a3b"],"keyword":["Best Practices","Diffusion of Innovation","Organizational Mechanisms","Technological Knowledge","Theory of Planned Behavior (TPB)"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-697AC1ABEB0FF96406659B547CF73C36"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Humanities (HUM)"],"yearPub":"2014","yearSub":"2014"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"ruc roskilde university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-RUC","l3nl":"Roskilde University","l3nn":"RUC Roskilde University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"RUC Roskilde University","namevar":["Roskilde University"],"nora_code":"DI-U-RUC","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"us","key":"georgia state university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Georgia State University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jan","key":"prieshejepau","last":"Pries-Heje","name":"Pries-Heje, Jan","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Richard","key":"baskervillepau","last":"Baskerville","name":"Baskerville, Richard","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Birgitta","key":"bergvallkårebornpedh","last":"Bergvall-Kåreborn","name":"Bergvall-Kåreborn, Birgitta","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Peter Axel","key":"nielsenpedh","last":"Nielsen","name":"Nielsen, Peter Axel","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-662-43459-8_3","isbn":["9783662434598"],"issn":["18684238"],"pages":"35-46","publisher":"Springer","series":"IFIP AICT - Advances in Information and Communication technology","status":"p","title":"Creating Value for All Through IT","volume":"429"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84927670859"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Roskilde University","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-961f1027-c258-4baa-b827-e889ac0ca00e","ruc-d9e7a194-08fa-4bb4-ae7f-e0e127d24a3b"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen","RUC Roskilde University"],"title":"Diffusing Best Practices : A Design Science Study Using the Theory of Planed Behavior","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-091.30-038350","year":"2014","yearsub":"2014"}
{"abstract":"While there are many possible approaches and areas of research for the study of social media, a review shows that only a few of these are used in extant information systems literature. The paper therefore suggests that increased awareness of the possibilities can strengthen research in social media. It is also suggested that information systems scholars can strengthen their interdisciplinary position by making the information systems discipline itself the home ground for research about social media. To address the issue of possible approaches and areas of research, the paper develops a framework that includes conceptual framing and methodology as well as research areas in the information systems discipline. First, the framework guides a review of extant literature about external social media. Going forward, the framework facilitates the development of a future research agenda about social media on information systems home ground.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Haahr, Lars"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-152183"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"wrestling with social media on information systems home ground","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007%2F978-3-319-09546-2_3","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-152183","identifier":[{"id":"84927601193","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-27d58ce3-0f23-41f4-b51b-da42483f8005"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-0E7BFDB6CFBEA3797FF0F43ED5CD14CF"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"http://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007%2F978-3-319-09546-2_3","version":"Other Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007%2F978-3-319-09546-2_3","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Aarhus BSS, Aarhus School of Business, Information Systems Research Group","Aarhus University, Aarhus BSS, Department of Management"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Lars","key":"haahrpau","last":"Haahr","locid":"e0ca185d-d58a-4e25-8cad-fafdfee3be26","name":"Haahr, Lars","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Trine Hald","key":"commissopedh","last":"Commisso","name":"Commisso, Trine Hald","rank":2,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Jacob","key":"nørbjergpedh","last":"Nørbjerg","name":"Nørbjerg, Jacob","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Jan","key":"prieshejepedh","last":"Pries-Heje","name":"Pries-Heje, Jan","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-319-09546-2_3","eisbn":["9783319095462"],"isbn":["9783319095455"],"issn":["18651348"],"pages":"30-43","publisher":"Springer","series":"Lecture Notes in Business Information Processing","status":"p","subtitle":"5th Scandinavian Conference on Information Systems, SCIS 2014, Ringsted, Denmark, August 10-13, 2014. Proceedings","title":"Nordic Contributions in IS Research","volume":"186"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84927601193"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-27d58ce3-0f23-41f4-b51b-da42483f8005"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Wrestling with Social Media on Information Systems’ Home Ground","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-152183","year":"2014","yearsub":"2014"}
{"abstract":"Technological knowledge has been characterized as having a scope that is specific to a particular problem. However, the information systems community is exploring forms of design science research that provide a promising avenue to technological knowledge with broader scope: design theories. Because design science research is materially prescriptive, it requires a different perspective in developing the breadth of applications of design theories. In this paper we propose different concepts that embody forms of general technological knowledge The concept of projectability, developed originally as a means of distinguishing realized generalizations from unrealized generalizations, helps explain how design theories, being prescriptive, possess a different form of applicability. The concept of entrenchment describes the use of a theory in many projections. Together these concepts provide a means for comparative discussions of the importance of design theories. Projectable design theories guide designers in the design of artifacts similar in principle, but different in context. These can also help design researchers understand interrelationships between design theories.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Pries-Heje, Jan","Baskerville, Richard"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-561772"],"event":{"acronym":"IFIP WG82","end":"2014-12-12","place":"Auckland, nz","start":"2014-12-11","title":"IFIP WG 8.2 Working Conference on Information Systems and Organizations","year":"2014"},"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Georgia State University"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-RUC"]},"level3all":["DI-U-RUC"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"design theory projectability","src":["ruc"],"src-code":["DI-U-RUC"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-561772","identifier":[{"id":"84915771184","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ruc-57059332-f452-4b71-b695-ada8f2dddc82"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities"],"noi":["Global-Local-3-5-B47354A924D1645194BF73947881DFC2"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Humanities (HUM)"],"yearPub":"2014","yearSub":"2014"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ruc roskilde university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-RUC","l3nl":"Roskilde University","l3nn":"RUC Roskilde University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"RUC Roskilde University","namevar":["Roskilde University, Department of People and Technology, User-Driven IT Innovation","Roskilde University, The Department of Communication, Business and Information Technologies"],"nora_code":"DI-U-RUC","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"georgia state university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Georgia State University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","first":"Jan","key":"prieshejepau","last":"Pries-Heje","locid":"6dbca1cc-ed5b-479e-be59-0cf0ae86a2a8","name":"Pries-Heje, Jan","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Richard","key":"baskervillepau","last":"Baskerville","name":"Baskerville, Richard","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"B.","key":"doolinpedh","last":"Doolin","name":"Doolin, B.","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"E.","key":"lamproupedh","last":"Lamprou","name":"Lamprou, E.","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"N.","key":"mitevpedh","last":"Mitev","name":"Mitev, N.","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"L.","key":"mcleodpedh","last":"McLeod","name":"McLeod, L.","rank":6,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-662-45708-5_14","isbn":["9783662457085"],"issn":["18684238"],"pages":"219-232","publisher":"Springer","series":"IFIP AICT - Advances in Information and Communication technology","status":"p","subtitle":"(Re)configuring Actors, Artefacts, Organizations","title":"Information Systems and Global Assemblages","volume":"446"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84915771184"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Roskilde University","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ruc-57059332-f452-4b71-b695-ada8f2dddc82"],"src":["RUC Roskilde University"],"title":"Design Theory Projectability","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-561772","year":"2014","yearsub":"2014"}
