","name":"Mikkelsen, Hans","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jens Ove","key":"riispau","last":"Riis","locid":"36a52a3c-964b-48d2-969c-d150e669a15b","name":"Riis, Jens Ove","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Jan","key":"prieshejepedh","last":"Pries-Heje","name":"Pries-Heje, Jan","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"edition":"1","isbn":["9788759317129"],"pages":"59-84","publisher":"Samfundslitteratur","status":"p","title":"Project Management Multiplicity: Current Trends"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-33706ce8-83e8-49c8-80f0-9b2d704b85c8"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"Projects and Company Development - Effectiveness and Productivity","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-064974","year":"2012","yearsub":"2012"}
{"abstract":"This chapter presents a communications approach to teaching project management analyzed from a Design Science Research perspective. Using a combination of product and process, as well as three inter-related Design Science Research Cycles—relevance, design and rigor—the Project Management Course in the Bachelor of Marketing and Management Communication at Aarhus University is examined and analyzed. The presence of the three Design Science Research Cycles combined with both process and product, highlights the critical role of communication in both understanding Project Management and applying Design Science Theory. In this case, the use of the Rigor cycle is grounded in Project Management tools and expertise, combined with Knowledge Management Theory. The Design cycle is focused on iterative feedback from both real clients and instructors. Finally, the Relevance cycle explains the use of real clients to both frame the learners’ audience for Project Management Writing assignments, and to provide a concrete organizational context to aid in communication practices such as stakeholder analysis, and the development of a critical internal monitor for quality in the learner’s mind. The relevance of Design Science Research cycles and concepts to communication is examined in the conclusion suggesting that investigating the role of communication in Design Science Theory Research cycles is a relevant direction for further study.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Kampf, Constance Elizabeth"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-202596"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Design science","Knowledge management","Project management"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a communications approach to teaching project management","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-202596","ids":["au-8018154a-0f00-4d8f-98c9-0fd32ec0e997"],"keyword":["Communciation","Design Science","Knowledge Management","Project Management"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Local-1-1-81E1DB7671BA1AF33621E8889BFE322B"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Aarhus BSS, Department of Management, Department of Management"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Constance Elizabeth","key":"kampfpau","last":"Kampf","locid":"044083e7-2350-49a1-a399-306ceab7248c","name":"Kampf, Constance Elizabeth","orcid":"0000-0003-4275-3978","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Jan","key":"prieshejepedh","last":"Pries-Heje","name":"Pries-Heje, Jan","rank":2,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"isbn":["9788759317129"],"place":"Kbh.","publisher":"Samfundslitteratur","status":"p","subtitle":"Current Trends","title":"Project Management Multiplicity"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-8018154a-0f00-4d8f-98c9-0fd32ec0e997"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"A Communications Approach to Teaching Project Management","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-202596","year":"2012","yearsub":"2012"}
{"abstract":"The notion of ‘Sociomateriality’ has recently gained in popularity among information systems (IS) scholars in their search for providing new ways of investigating and theorizing about IS in organizations and society at large. While some scholars put forward arguments and research accounts that lead to new insights, others expose a cursory treatment and partial appreciation of the idea. In addition, sociomateriality as a new worldview has been criticized for introducing yet more academic jargon monoxide. Although existing research and debates show some potential for progressing the theorizing of ‘man-machine’ reconfigurations, we point to the necessity of a deeper exploration of the term. Inspired by the call for papers of the Alternative Genres Track at ECIS 2012, the purpose is to “take a fresh look, to evoke new insights and to gain deeper understanding” of the notion of sociomateriality in IS. Towards this aim, the reader is invited to attend a prolonged monologue – characterized by honesty, frank observations and wit – delivered by the court jester and directed to the sovereigns based on their writings. The intention is to contribute with a stimulating recital that builds on open-minded questions in the pursuit of key reflections to inform our research discipline.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Kautz, Karlheinz","Jensen, Tina Blegind"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-298045"],"event":{"end":"2012-06-13","place":"Barcelona, es","start":"2012-06-10","title":"20th European Conference on Information Systems, ECIS 2012","year":"2012"},"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Imbrication","Information systems","Sociomateriality"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-CBS"]},"level3all":["DI-U-CBS"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"sociomateriality more than jargon monoxide questions from the jester to the sovereigns","src":["cbs"],"src-code":["DI-U-CBS"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"http://aisel.aisnet.org/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1053&context=ecis2012","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-298045","identifier":[{"id":"84905750477","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["cbs-eda6fe6a-bd9b-4b04-a398-9da290df027d"],"keyword":["Alternative genre","Imbrication","Information systems","Sociomateriality"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-FA70C84D758FF5F3673BC9E4AD91A3D2"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"cbs copenhagen business school","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-CBS","l3nl":"Copenhagen Business School","l3nn":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","namevar":["Copenhagen Business School, Department of Operations Management"],"nora_code":"DI-U-CBS","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Karlheinz","key":"kautzpau","last":"Kautz","locid":"13394c0e-7e07-4611-98fa-d8958fdfa497","name":"Kautz, Karlheinz","orcid":"0000-0003-3597-8781","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Tina Blegind","key":"jensenpau","last":"Jensen","locid":"5733d9c1-e4a9-426d-84bb-a5d5dd1a8973","name":"Jensen, Tina Blegind","orcid":"0000-0003-3095-1344","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Jan","key":"prieshejepedh","last":"Pries-Heje","name":"Pries-Heje, Jan","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Mike","key":"chiassonpedh","last":"Chiasson","name":"Chiasson, Mike","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Jonathan","key":"warehampedh","last":"Wareham","name":"Wareham, Jonathan","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Xavier","key":"busquetspedh","last":"Busquets","name":"Busquets, Xavier","rank":6,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Josep","key":"valorpedh","last":"Valor","name":"Valor, Josep","rank":7,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Sandra","key":"seiberpedh","last":"Seiber","name":"Seiber, Sandra","rank":8,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"eisbn":["9788488971548"],"issn":["00000034"],"place":"Atlanta, GA","publisher":"Association for Information Systems. AIS Electronic Library (AISeL)","series":"Proceedings of the European Conference on Information Systems","status":"p","title":"ECIS 2012 Proceedings"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84905750477"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","Copenhagen Business School","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["cbs-eda6fe6a-bd9b-4b04-a398-9da290df027d"],"src":["CBS Copenhagen Business School"],"title":"Sociomateriality - More than Jargon Monoxide? : Questions from the Jester to the Sovereigns","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-298045","year":"2012","yearsub":"2012"}
{"abstract":"Activities and initiatives of co-creation are traditionally seen as a way for organizations to gain value through the involvement of certain actors in their environment. We notice an implicit assumption in current theoretical conceptualizations that co-creation is initiated and driven exclusively by organizations. However, it appears that co-creation activities may also be driven by third-party actors outside organizations. Based on interviews and secondary data from a public transport company in Stockholm, Sweden, we noticed that third party developers of services, that gained a large and diverse user base, were driving co-creation activities with the respective organization. Subsequently, based on our findings, we introduce the term \"crowdpushing\" to denote externally driven co-creation activities and frame four propositions to describe how co-creation activities are motivated and driven. Our findings contribute to a broader understanding of co-creation and have implications for its design and deployment.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Rudmark, Daniel","Arnestrand, Elias","Avital, Michel"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-299460"],"event":{"end":"2012-06-13","place":"Barcelona, es","start":"2012-06-10","title":"20th European Conference on Information Systems, ECIS 2012","year":"2012"},"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Högskolan i Borås","Viktoria Institute"],"keyword":["Co creation","Innovation","Open data","Public transport","Third Party Development"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-CBS"]},"level3all":["DI-U-CBS"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"crowdpushing the flip side of crowdsourcing","src":["cbs"],"src-code":["DI-U-CBS"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft